  "readTime": 1,
  "content": "<table align=\"center\" border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\">\n<tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em;\">\n<div>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr>\n<td align=\"left\" style=\"padding-left: 4px; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em;\"><p>#​675 — November 25, 2026</p></td>\n<td align=\"right\" style=\"padding-right: 4px; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em;\"><p><a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/193425/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb;\" target=\"_blank\">Web Version</a></p></td>\n</tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em;\"></td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0 12px;\"><p>Postgres Weekly</p></td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr style=\"line-height: 1.0em;\"><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.0em;\">\n<a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/193351/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; line-height: 1.0em;\" target=\"_blank\"><img loading=\"lazy\" referrerpolicy=\"no-referrer\" src=\"https://res.cloudinary.com/cpress/image/upload/w_1280,e_sharpen:60,q_auto/mjxv2zfstxmfeqeu2pnj.jpg\" style=\"line-height: 1.0em;\" width=\"640\"/></a>\n</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px 15px;\">\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 600; font-size: 1.1em; color: #000;\"><a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/193351/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; font-size: 1.1em; line-height: 1.4em;\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"github.com\">Postgres.app 3.0 Brings Easy Postgres 19 to the Mac</a></span> — If you're not on macOS, keep scrolling, but otherwise this is a neat release of an easy way to run Postgres on the Mac. 3.0 adds Postgres 19 and PostGIS 3.7 and has an updated UI <em>(above)</em>. You can grab it as a 19 only build or one that includes 14 through 19.</p>\n<p>Jakob Egger et al. </p>\n</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px 15px;\"><p>💡 Note that v3.0 has 'unbundled' some previously installed extensions like <a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/193352/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; font-weight: 600;\" target=\"_blank\">pgRouting</a> and <a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/193353/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; font-weight: 600;\" target=\"_blank\">wal2json</a>. These are now available as <a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/193354/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; font-weight: 600;\" target=\"_blank\">separate downloads</a>.</p></td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px 15px;\">\n<a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/193350/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb;\" target=\"_blank\"><img height=\"110\" loading=\"lazy\" referrerpolicy=\"no-referrer\" src=\"https://res.cloudinary.com/cpress/image/upload/c_limit,w_480,h_480,q_auto/copm/c61ebe9e.png\" style=\"padding-top: 12px; padding-left: 12px; line-height: 100%;\" width=\"110\"/></a>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 600; font-size: 1.1em; color: #000;\"><a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/193350/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; font-size: 1.05em;\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"www.tigerdata.com\">Stop Scanning Rows You Don’t Need</a></span> — As your Postgres tables grow, queries slow down and indexing alone can only do so much. TimescaleDB automatically partitions data by time, allowing Postgres to skip irrelevant chunks and query only what it needs. Faster queries, less tuning, and the same Postgres you already know.</p>\n<p>Tiger Data (creators of TimescaleDB) <span style=\"margin-left: 4px; font-size: 0.9em; color: #997 !important; padding: 1px 4px;\">sponsor</span></p>\n</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px 15px;\">\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 600; font-size: 1.1em; color: #000;\"><a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/193355/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; font-size: 1.05em;\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"www.cybertec-postgresql.com\">Forcing a Join Order in Postgres 19 with <code>pg_stash_advice</code></a></span> — Postgres 19's plan advice modules give us the closest (official) thing to query hints so far. Laurenz tests a join order that takes a query from 405ms to ~10ms, stores that advice under the query ID, but also argues for using this approach sparingly.</p>\n<p>Laurenz Albe (CYBERTEC) </p>\n</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px 15px;\"><p>💡 I say 'official' because there are other ways to do <em>similar</em> things with extensions like <a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/193356/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; font-weight: 600;\" target=\"_blank\">pg_hint_plan</a> which uses Oracle-like hints and now has <a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/193357/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; font-weight: 600;\" target=\"_blank\">a Postgres 19 release</a>.</p></td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px 15px;\">\n<p><strong>IN BRIEF:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>If you use Docker's <code>postgres:latest</code>, bear in mind <a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/193358/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; font-weight: 500 !important;\" target=\"_blank\">it's now switched to Postgres 19</a>. If your volume holds a v18 database, the updated container will error, and you should pin <code>postgres:18</code> <a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/193359/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; font-weight: 500 !important;\" target=\"_blank\">until you've upgraded</a>.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>🇨🇦 The popular <a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/193360/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; font-weight: 500 !important;\" target=\"_blank\">PGConf.dev is back</a> across May 11–14, 2027 in Montréal, and <a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/193361/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; font-weight: 500 !important;\" target=\"_blank\">its CFP is open</a> (until January 12).</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>🇸🇪 While on the other side of the pond, <a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/193362/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; font-weight: 500 !important;\" target=\"_blank\">Nordic PGDay 2027</a> takes place March 16 in Stockholm. <a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/193363/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; font-weight: 500 !important;\" target=\"_blank\">The CFP is open</a> till January 8.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Robert Haas and Jonathan Katz <a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/193364/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; font-weight: 500 !important;\" target=\"_blank\">revisit why Postgres 19 shipped late</a>.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px 15px;\">\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 600; font-size: 1.1em; color: #000;\"><a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/193365/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; font-size: 1.05em;\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"pgcraftsman.github.io\">The Postgres Mistakes We All Still Make</a></span> — Slides from a NYC meetup on mistakes or misassumptions (delightfully zero indexed!) that can show up at scale, covering MVCC quirks, open transactions, bulk deletes and unused indexes. There's also <a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/193366/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb;\" target=\"_blank\">a repo</a> with SQL showing off the problems.</p>\n<p>Scott Mead (Databricks) </p>\n</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px 15px;\">\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 600; font-size: 1.1em; color: #000;\"><a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/193367/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; font-size: 1.05em;\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"percona.community\">What's Left in Postgres 19 After the Reverts</a></span> — Postgres 19 lost a variety of anticipated features late on, but what <em>did</em> make it? Zsolt tours the survivors.</p>\n<p>Zsolt Parragi (Percona) </p>\n</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px 15px;\">\n<p>📄 <a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/193368/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; font-weight: 500 !important;\" target=\"_blank\">Squeezing Tables with Postgres 19</a> – The inside story of how <code>REPACK (CONCURRENTLY)</code> made it into v19, from the committer who fought to keep it in. <cite>Álvaro Herrera (EDB)</cite></p>\n<p>📄 <a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/193369/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; font-weight: 500 !important;\" target=\"_blank\">How to Deal with Slow Postgres Shutdowns</a> – You'd think <em>stopping</em> Postgres would be faster than starting it, but no, and Laurenz looks at why. <cite>Laurenz Albe (CYBERTEC)</cite></p>\n</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em;\"></td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px;\">\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em;\"></td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px 15px;\">\n<p>📰 Classifieds</p>\n</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px 15px;\">\n<p>⚡ Already on Postgres? <a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/193370/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; font-weight: 500 !important;\" target=\"_blank\">Rebase</a> adds REST, a typed SDK, auth, storage, functions and cron, plus a ready admin panel. MIT licensed.</p>\n</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em;\"></td></tr></table>\n</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em;\"></td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px 15px;\">\n<p><strong>RELEASES AND CODE:</strong></p>\n</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px 15px;\">\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 600; font-size: 1.1em; color: #000;\"><a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/193371/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; font-size: 1.05em;\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"github.com\">PGlite 0.6: Postgres 19, Compiled to WebAssembly</a></span> — The WASM build of Postgres for the browser (plus Node and Bun) now runs Postgres 19, so trying out the new features in your tests, say, is only an <code>npm install</code> away. Persisted data from older versions needs a <a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/193372/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb;\" target=\"_blank\">dump and restore</a>.</p>\n<p>ElectricSQL </p>\n</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px 15px;\"><p>💡 No blog post yet so the link above is to the rather spartan release notes, but if you're new to PGlite <a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/193426/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; font-weight: 600;\" target=\"_blank\">the docs</a> are a good place to start.</p></td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px 15px;\">\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 600; font-size: 1.1em; color: #000;\"><a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/193373/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; font-size: 1.05em;\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"github.com\">gopgext: Write Postgres Extensions in Go</a></span> — Last week we covered Rust's <a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/193374/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb;\" target=\"_blank\">pgrx</a>, now it's Go's turn! With this, you can mark Go functions with a comment and it'll build them into a Postgres extension using <a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/193375/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb;\" target=\"_blank\">TinyGo</a> with the C glue code and SQL included. It's very new and experimental, however.</p>\n<p>Jesús Espino </p>\n</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px 15px;\"><p>💡 If you're into Go, check out our sister newsletter <a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/193376/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; font-weight: 600;\" target=\"_blank\">Go Weekly</a> :-)</p></td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px 15px;\">\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 600; font-size: 1.1em; color: #000;\"><a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/193377/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; font-size: 1.05em;\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"github.com\">PgJDBC 42.7.15: Many Security Fixes for the Java/JDBC Driver</a></span> — The official JDBC driver closes fourteen security <a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/193378/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb;\" target=\"_blank\">advisories</a> (six rated high) in one go with some of the bugs going back years.</p>\n<p>pgjdbc </p>\n</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px 15px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/193379/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; font-weight: 500 !important; font-size: 1.08em; line-height: 1.5em;\" target=\"_blank\">WAL-G 3.0.10</a> – A feature-rich release for the backup/recovery tool, including a new <code>wal-send</code> command that lets standbys stream WAL from backup storage.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/193380/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; font-weight: 500 !important; font-size: 1.08em; line-height: 1.5em;\" target=\"_blank\">pg_parquet 0.6</a> – Crunchy Data's extension for <a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/193381/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; font-weight: 500 !important;\" target=\"_blank\">copying to and from Parquet files</a> now supports Postgres 19.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>🔎 <a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/193382/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; font-weight: 500 !important; font-size: 1.08em; line-height: 1.5em;\" target=\"_blank\">pg_textsearch 1.6</a> – Tiger Data's BM25 ranked text search extension also joins the Postgres 19 train, plus has some efficiency and ranking improvements.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/193383/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; font-weight: 500 !important; font-size: 1.08em; line-height: 1.5em;\" target=\"_blank\">PGSync 8.0</a> – Postgres to Elasticsearch/OpenSearch syncing tool.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/193384/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; font-weight: 500 !important; font-size: 1.08em; line-height: 1.5em;\" target=\"_blank\">Npgsql 11.0</a> – The ADO.NET data provider for Postgres.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em;\"></td></tr></table>\n</div>\n</td></tr>\n</table>\n",
  "attachments": []
}